        "note": "<div class=\"Example\"><table class=\"example\"><tr><td><p><i>e̩</i></p></td><td><p>2nd sg. hon.</p></td><td><p>- the 2nd pl subject pronoun is used for a polite address; it isused to address a superior individual, an older person, or a personof higher social status</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><i>è̩yin</i></p></td><td><p>2nd sg. hon.</p></td><td><p>- this is a form of pronominal which belongs to a series of freepronouns which occur exclusively in non-verbal clauses and beforeverbs such as <i>dà</i> ‘where is?’, <i>´nkó̩</i> ‘what about?’etc.;</p><p>- in other structures, pronominals can replace ordinary pronounsand then indicate emphasis;</p><p>- these pronominals behave more like nouns than likepronouns;</p><p>- used to address a superior individual, an older person or aperson of higher social status</p></td></tr></table></div>",
        "scale": "wals-v2020.5"
      }
    ]
